From feldesmanm at pdx.edu  Mon Jun  1 19:27:11 1998
From: feldesmanm at pdx.edu (Marc Feldesman)
Date: Mon, 01 Jun 1998 12:27:11 -0700
Subject: [S] Beginner question with apply & tapply
Message-ID: <3.0.5.32.19980601122711.007a06e0@pop4.ibm.net>

I'm still learning the subleties and nuances of the apply() and tapply()
functions, so please forgive what might be a naive question.  I've worked
my way through both VR and Spector but can't find a specific example or
hint that can be generalized to my particular problem.

The problem:

I have a dataset (a data frame to be exact) with 8 variables.  One of the
variables is a grouping variable, called appropriately enough "group".  The
data were obtained years ago and were entered in acquisition order, not in
group order.  The file is not sorted by group, and the number of
individuals per group is unequal (I know the dataset well enough to know
that there are four groups).  I am interested in computing the
variance-covariance matrix for 4 of the variables for each group.

Suppose:

headdata=data.frame
headdata[,2:5] are variables of interest
headdata[,8] is the grouping variable

apply(headdata[,2:5], 2, function(x) tapply(x, headdata[,8], var))

gives me the variances for each variable in each group, but does not give
me the vcv. 

There is an example in VR using the iris data (to do exactly what I'm
trying above) that I've been unable to generalize because the data are not
in a list structure and the sample sizes are unequal.  There is also an
example in Spector that is generalized above, but the more I look at
Spector's example and my code above (plus understanding how "var" works),
the less I think this particular approach will work.

So, my question is:

Are "apply" and "tapply" the right beasts for this kind of problem.  I come
from a world in which looping would be the preferred strategy, but I've had
it drummed into my head so far that vectorized calculations are faster and
more efficient.  If they are the right approach, what step(s) am I missing
in my understanding.

If they are not, I'll stop pursuing this strategy and consider other
approaches (e.g. looping, design matrices, etc).

Thanks for your help/suggestions/abuse.
